title: Shifting bits along
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 100
---- intro
A **shift register** moves its bits one place at every clock edge, and a new bit `din` comes in at the bottom. After four ticks, the last four bits that came in are all there. It is how a serial link, one wire carrying one bit at a time, turns into a byte.

The shift is the slicing and joining you already know: the low three bits, with `din` joined on the right.

```
s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;
```

An output cannot be read inside the architecture, so the bits are kept in a signal `s`, declared for you, and `q` is connected to it.
---- task
At every rising edge of `clk`, shift `s` one place to the left with `din` coming in at bit 0. `q` shows `s`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity shift4 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    din : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end shift4;

architecture rtl of shift4 is
    signal s : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity shift4 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    din : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end shift4;

architecture rtl of shift4 is
    signal s : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;
        end if;
    end process;
    q <= s;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two things: a clock process that shifts `s`, and a line outside it that connects `q` to `s`.
---- hint2
Inside `if rising_edge(clk) then`: `s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;`. After `end process;`: `q <= s;`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;
    end if;
end process;
q <= s;
```
Set `din` to 1 and Step once, then 0 and Step again: the 1 moves left one place each tick.
---- done
Bits go in one at a time and come out side by side. Next: loading it all at once.
